UPDATE companies SET description='Maine Crisp Company operates from Winslow, Maine, where founder Karen launched the business after a career that spanned bread baking, dairy farming, and cheesemaking. Unable to find gluten-free crackers that met her standards for ingredients, taste, and texture, she began developing her own buckwheat-based recipes. The company is now transitioning to the Better with Buckwheat brand.

The facility produces a line of gluten-free, grain-free crackers made from buckwheat, a seed cultivated for over 8,000 years. Current flavors include Cranberry Almond, Everything, Savory Fig & Thyme, and Sea Salt. Production is described as handmade, though the company does not disclose facility size, headcount, or annual volume.

Customers range from individual consumers to food-service accounts. Chefs at Union Restaurant in Portland and Alice and Lulu''s in Sugarloaf Village have endorsed the crackers, as has C Salt Gourmet Market in Cape Elizabeth. The product is also sold direct through the company''s website.

The company emphasizes buckwheat''s environmental profile: it grows in low-fertility soils, regenerates the land, and supports pollinators—one acre can sustain a full hive. Nutritionally, the crackers are positioned as low-glycemic, anti-inflammatory, and high in fiber. Karen''s original motivation was personal: creating a snack she could enjoy daily without compromising on flavor or ingredient integrity.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Gluten-free snack manufacturing", "Grain-free baking", "Buckwheat cracker production", "Small-batch handmade production", "Food service supply", "Direct-to-consumer fulfillment"], "industries": ["Gluten-free foods", "Snack food manufacturing", "Specialty food retail", "Food service", "Natural and organic foods"], "materials": ["Buckwheat", "Cranberry", "Almond", "Everything seasoning", "Fig", "Thyme", "Sea salt"], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Handmade in Maine", "Gluten-free and grain-free formulation", "Buckwheat as a regenerative, pollinator-friendly crop", "Low-glycemic, anti-inflammatory, high-fiber profile", "Direct-to-consumer and food-service distribution"], "facts_used": ["Founder Karen started company after careers in bread baking, dairy farming, cheesemaking -> /", "Company based in Winslow, Maine -> db", "Produces gluten-free, grain-free buckwheat crackers in multiple flavors -> /", "Rebranding to Better with Buckwheat -> /", "Handmade production described -> /", "Customers include Union Restaurant (Portland), Alice and Lulu''s (Sugarloaf Village), C Salt Gourmet Market (Cape Elizabeth) -> /", "Buckwheat described as regenerative crop supporting pollinators -> /", "Health claims: low glycemic, anti-inflammatory, high fiber -> /"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "Handmade in Maine", "founded_year": "", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='maine-crisp-company' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Maine Printing & Embroidery operates from Warren, a coastal community in Maine''s Mid Coast region. The family‑owned shop has been in business for more than 40 years, though a specific founding year is not published.

The facility houses both manual and automatic screen printing presses, enabling runs from a few custom tees to orders of several hundred pieces. In addition to screen printing, the shop offers embroidery, CAD‑cut vinyl graphics, banner and decal production, vehicle wraps, and general signage. An in‑house art department staffed with several artists handles original artwork, custom shirt designs, and vehicle‑wrap layouts, keeping all design and production under one roof.

The customer base includes businesses and organizations needing branded apparel, promotional items, fleet graphics, and interior or exterior signage. Small orders of just a couple of shirts are accommodated through vinyl cut graphics, while larger volumes move to screen printing. The shop also serves clients requiring vehicle identification, wall graphics, and business identification signage.

